# AgentForge — Cursor IDE Rules
# These rules guide Claude in Cursor to generate correct, production-quality code for this project.

## Project Context
This is a Python AI agent built on LangGraph that provides financial portfolio analysis
by integrating with the Ghostfolio REST API. The agent uses Claude (Anthropic) as the
primary LLM and runs a 5-stage verification pipeline on every response.

## Architecture Rules

### Always
- Use LangGraph `StateGraph` with `AgentState` TypedDict for all agent logic
- Use `async/await` for all tool implementations (tools call `asyncio.run()` for sync compatibility)
- Use `pydantic-settings` for all configuration — never hardcode secrets or URLs
- Use `httpx.AsyncClient` for all HTTP calls inside `GhostfolioClient`
- Add `data_timestamp` (ISO 8601 UTC) to every tool response dict
- Return `{"status": "error", "error": "..."}` on failures — never raise unhandled exceptions in tools
- Run all 5 verifiers via `run_verification_pipeline()` before returning any response to the user
- Use `tenacity` `@retry` decorator on all Ghostfolio API calls
- Import tools only from `agent.tools` (via `ALL_TOOLS` list)

### Never
- Never hardcode API keys, tokens, or URLs — use `agent.config.settings`
- Never make LLM calls inside tool functions — tools are pure data fetchers
- Never skip the verification pipeline — every user-facing response must pass through it
- Never use `requests` (sync) — always use `httpx` (async)
- Never return raw exceptions to users — catch and return structured error dicts
- Never use `print()` for logging — use `structlog`

## Testing Rules

### TDD Discipline
- Write the failing test FIRST, then implement
- Every tool function must have unit tests with mocked HTTP (use `respx`)
- Every verification function must have unit tests (pure Python, no mocks needed)
- Coverage must stay above 85% — CI will fail below this threshold
- Test files mirror source structure: `tests/unit/tools/` for `agent/tools/`

### Test Patterns
- Use `@respx.mock` decorator for all httpx mocking in tool tests
- Use `pytest.fixture` in `conftest.py` for shared mock API responses
- Use `unittest.mock.patch` for yfinance mocking
- Always test: happy path, empty/null input, network error, invalid input

## Code Style

### Python
- Python 3.11+
- Type hints on all function signatures
- `from __future__ import annotations` at top of every file
- Docstrings on all tools (the LLM reads these to understand when to use each tool)
- Max line length: 100 characters
- Use `ruff` for linting

### LangGraph Patterns
- Nodes return `dict` with only the state keys they modify
- Use `add_messages` annotation for the `messages` field
- Conditional edges use string routing keys matching node names
- Always set `set_entry_point()` on the graph

### Tool Docstrings (Critical)
Tool docstrings are read by the LLM to decide when to use each tool.
They must clearly describe:
1. WHAT the tool does
2. WHEN to use it (natural language query examples)
3. What each parameter means
4. What the return dict contains

## File Organization
```
agent/
  config.py          ← Settings only, no business logic
  prompts.py         ← System prompt and few-shot examples
  tools/             ← One file per tool, @tool decorated functions
  verification/      ← __init__.py contains all 5 verifiers + pipeline
  graph/
    state.py         ← AgentState TypedDict definition
    graph.py         ← Graph assembly, nodes, edges, routing
  clients/
    ghostfolio.py    ← GhostfolioClient async HTTP client
    market.py        ← MarketDataClient (yfinance)
  api/               ← FastAPI routes and schemas
  ui/                ← Chainlit app
tests/
  conftest.py        ← Shared fixtures
  unit/tools/        ← Tool unit tests
  unit/verification/ ← Verifier unit tests
  integration/       ← Full graph flow tests
  adversarial/       ← Safety and prompt injection tests
  eval/              ← LangSmith eval dataset and runner
```

## Verification Pipeline (Non-Negotiable)
All 5 checks run on EVERY response in this order:
1. `check_disclaimer` — adds "Not financial advice" for investment language
2. `check_hallucination` — flags numbers not sourced from tool calls
3. `check_freshness` — flags stale market data (>15min) or portfolio data (>60min)
4. `check_concentration` — flags positions >20% of portfolio
5. `check_confidence` — assigns HIGH/MEDIUM/LOW confidence score

## LLM Configuration
- Primary: `claude-sonnet-4-5` via `langchain-anthropic`
- Fallback: `gpt-4o` via `langchain-openai`
- Temperature: 0 (deterministic for financial data)
- Always bind tools to the LLM: `llm.bind_tools(ALL_TOOLS)`

## Common Mistakes to Avoid
- Do not use `LangChain AgentExecutor` — use LangGraph `StateGraph`
- Do not import tools individually — import from `agent.tools.ALL_TOOLS`
- Do not forget `asyncio.run()` wrapper in `@tool` functions (LangChain tools are sync)
- Do not skip `data_timestamp` in tool responses (verification layer needs it)
- Do not use `WidthType.PERCENTAGE` in any table (DXA only)
